clarity_hierarchy.raw_response:
{
"critique":"The primary focal point is fractured. The large, high-contrast 'CRATEWORKS' title in the top-left competes directly with the puzzle board for initial attention, burying the actual gameplay. The 'Objectives' bar is visually noisy; the three items are evenly weighted, and the 'PENDING' and 'LOCKED' status tags are small, low-contrast, and disconnected from the main action, making the current goal ambiguous. The board itself suffers from flat hierarchy: the player, the crate, and the target pad all have similar visual weight, making it difficult to instantly distinguish the 'actor' from the 'object' and the 'goal'. The target pad's glowing border is a strong visual element, but it is not clearly linked to the crate it needs to receive. The 'Help & Legend' panel on the right is a large, dense block of text that creates a heavy visual mass, competing with the board and disrupting the left-to-right reading flow.",
"suggestion":"1. Reduce the scale and contrast of the 'CRATEWORKS' title to make the puzzle board the undisputed primary focal point. 2. Reorganize the 'Objectives' bar to highlight the *current* objective (e.g., 'Seat Crate') with a larger font and brighter color, while de-emphasizing future objectives ('Flip Lever', 'Exit') with smaller, dimmer text. 3. Increase the visual prominence of the player character (e.g., a subtle glow or outline) to clearly establish them as the primary actor. 4. Create a visual link between the crate and the target pad, such as a faint, dashed line or a directional indicator, to clarify the goal relationship. 5. Collapse the 'Help & Legend' panel into a smaller, less intrusive element (e.g., a single icon or a compact, two-column layout) to reduce its visual weight and free up space for the board."
}

visual_system_craft.raw_response:
{
"critique":"The board's visual system is fundamentally broken: the top wall row is rendered as a continuous, unsegmented dark band that ignores the 8-column grid, while the floor tiles below are clearly gridded, creating a jarring construction mismatch. The wall tiles also have a heavy vertical gradient that does not match the flat, subtle floor tiles, so the two materials read as unrelated. The lever is oversized and its base plate overflows its cell, colliding with the grid lines and breaking the one-entity-per-cell grammar. The player sprite is a flat 2D icon that lacks the volumetric, beveled, shadowed treatment of the crate and lever, making it look like a placeholder. The HUD stat cards (Level/Moves/Best) have inconsistent internal alignment and the '01 / 12' and '0 · par 8' baselines do not sit on a shared optical line. The objective pills in the board header have uneven internal padding and the 'PENDING'/'LOCKED' badges are not vertically centered against the labels. The legend icons are tiny and low-contrast, and the right panel's two-column legend has ragged, unaligned text baselines. The overall palette is muddy and low-contrast, with the floor tiles nearly indistinguishable from the background, weakening the puzzle's legibility.",
"suggestion":"1) Rebuild the board as a strict, uniform grid: give every cell—including walls—the same tile size, border, and corner radius, and apply a consistent material (subtle top-light gradient + 1px inner stroke) to all tiles so walls and floor read as one system. 2) Constrain every entity to its cell: shrink the lever so its base fits within the tile, and give the player a beveled, shadowed 3D form matching the crate/lever material language. 3) Establish a spacing and alignment token set (4/8px scale) and apply it to the HUD cards, objective pills, and legend so labels, values, and badges share consistent baselines and padding. 4) Increase floor-tile contrast against the board background and add a faint grid highlight on hover/active cells to improve puzzle legibility. 5) Unify the icon set: render legend and objective icons at a consistent size and weight with the same stroke and fill treatment as the board entities. 6) Tighten typography: align all HUD numerals on a shared baseline, use tabular figures, and ensure the 'par' and 'session' sublabels share the same muted color and size."
}

expressiveness.raw_response:
